{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Fiscal rules to balance the budget and reduce debt; no rises in NI/Income Tax/VAT; New Border Security Command and plan to reduce net migration with tougher enforcement; unshakeable NATO and nuclear deterrent, path to 2.5% defence; Neighbourhood Policing Guarantee, tougher shoplifting rules, knife bans, expand prison capacity; pro-business industrial strategy, corporation tax capped at 25% and full expensing; NHS reforms using independent sector capacity, more scanners, shared waiting lists. TEXT_C: End routine stop-and-search and facial recognition; decriminalise sex work; presumption against short prison terms; sign UN nuclear ban, cancel Trident, reform NATO with no-first-use; end hostile environment and NRPF, relax spousal income rules, voting rights for visa holders; wealth tax, align CGT with earnings, 10:1 pay ratio cap, extensive public ownership, rent controls, UBI ambition; large NHS/social care expansions and end privatisation.",
  "reasoning": "Because the electorate profile prioritises authority, proportionality, and care, TEXT_E aligns by stressing security, borders, NATO/nuclear deterrence, tougher policing, fiscal rules and pro-business policies alongside pragmatic NHS improvements. TEXT_C places stronger weight on equality/redistribution and adopts positions that weaken authority/loyalty signals (policing rollbacks, anti-nuclear stance, liberal migration), which are less aligned with the profile’s top foundations.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
